                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>That's the rebalanced funnel.</strong> Fund internal where Lumen genuinely leads, license in for speed, out-license the dormant periphery — open innovation as a portfolio decision, not a slogan. This is the budget shape that both accelerates Tritón and starts monetising the shelf.`,
                    coach_r2_closed:        `<strong>“Openness is a slogan, not a budget line” keeps the funnel welded shut.</strong> If the €19.2M still flows entirely through internal projects, nothing has changed since the day AquaShield beat you. A turnaround the board can see has to move money across the boundary, not just the org chart.`,
                    coach_r2_outsource:     `<strong>Maximum outsourcing is over-correction, not open innovation.</strong> Shrink internal R&D too hard and you lose <em>absorptive capacity</em> — the ability to recognise and use external ideas. A firm that can't evaluate what it licenses in becomes a buyer of other people's leftovers.`,
                    coach_r2_allinternal:   `<strong>Keeping ~100% of the budget internal is the closed model with a new label.</strong> You can call it open innovation in the deck, but the money tells the truth — and the money never crossed the boundary. This reverts to exactly how Lumen lost the race.`,
                    coach_r2_hollow:        `<strong>You've starved internal R&D to fund openness.</strong> Without enough in-house capability, you can't tell a good external idea from a bad one — absorptive capacity collapses. Open innovation augments the internal engine; it doesn't replace it.`,
                    coach_r2_nopartner:     `<strong>A rebalanced budget with no partner is openness on paper only.</strong> Inbound flows through relationships — a spin-out, a shipyard, an accelerator. Pick no one and the external ideas have no door to come through. Who, concretely, feeds your pipeline next quarter?`,
                    coach_r2_deal_yes:      `<strong>Signing the inbound enzyme deal is the move the whole crisis turned on.</strong> €2.8M upfront and a 6% royalty buys you a launch in 8 months and a real shot at the first-mover share. The decision to open is made — next round you have to make the <em>terms</em> protect it.`,
                    coach_r2_deal_nospinout:`<strong>You said yes to the Tritón deal but didn't anchor on the spin-out.</strong> The enzyme that beat you sits with that spin-out specifically — co-developing around it without securing it leaves the one asset AquaShield is also chasing exposed. Build the network around the partner that actually holds the key.`,
                    coach_r2_deal_no:       `<strong>Refusing the €2.8M licence sends Tritón back to the 14-month internal path.</strong> “Too much to pay outsiders for our flagship” is the not-invented-here reflex with a price tag. The €2.8M buys six months of first-mover share worth far more than that. You re-closed the door you'd just cracked open.`,
                    coach_r3_nonexclusive:  `<strong>A non-exclusive licence with no field-of-use clause is opening without protection.</strong> The spin-out is now free to license the identical enzyme to AquaShield next week — and they will. You bought speed and zero defence. Value capture isn't in the decision to license; it's in the term sheet you just left blank.`,
                    coach_r3_exclusive_all: `<strong>Exclusive across every field over-buys.</strong> Locking up the enzyme for medical, aerospace and fields Lumen will never enter is paying a premium for shelf space. Carve the field-of-use to marine where you compete — protect what matters, don't bankroll the rest.`,
                    coach_r3_outbound_periphery: `<strong>That's the side door, opened on purpose.</strong> Licensing ≥3 peripheral dormant patents to non-competing adjacent players turns dead assets into a revenue stream — the €4M/yr AquaShield already earns — while the anti-corrosion core stays sealed. Outbound openness, done with a scalpel.`,
                    coach_r3_outbound_core: `<strong>Out-licensing the anti-corrosion know-how is a fire sale, not openness.</strong> That capability is the one customers buy Lumen for. In two years your licensee is your competitor, with your technology, at a lower price. Liberate the periphery — never the core.`,
                    coach_r3_outbound_none: `<strong>Keeping all 140 patents in-house leaves the side of the funnel welded shut.</strong> 98 of them have earned zero for years and will keep doing so. “Monetising looks like weakness” is the not-invented-here syndrome pointed inward. The dormant shelf is your easiest open-innovation win.`,
                    coach_r3_allcore:       `<strong>“Everything is core” is hoarding dressed as caution.</strong> If no asset can ever leave, the 98 dormant patents stay dead and the side of the funnel never opens. The discipline of open innovation is precisely drawing the line — not refusing to draw it.`,
                    coach_r3_allperiphery:  `<strong>“Almost nothing is truly core” erases the line you most need.</strong> Cash beats secrecy until the day your licensee underprices you with your own anti-corrosion technology. Some know-how is the reason Lumen exists; sell that and openness becomes self-harm.`,
                    coach_r3_escalation_option: `<strong>You moved on the spin-out before Friday.</strong> A binding option or co-investment secures the inbound door against AquaShield's exclusive bid — and keeps the medical-coatings segment in play. Open-innovation windows are time-bound; you treated this one like the contested asset it is.`,
                    coach_r3_escalation_walk: `<strong>Walking away hands AquaShield the spin-out.</strong> If their exclusive bid closes, the enzyme door slams and a second premium segment goes with it. “We'll find another route” is what you said about Tritón before they beat you. Contested partners don't wait.`,
                    coach_r3_escalation_ignore: `<strong>While you deliberated, AquaShield put an exclusive offer on the spin-out's table.</strong> If it closes Friday your inbound door is gone and the medical-coatings segment falls to the same rival. Open-innovation partners are contested assets — you watched a second segment slip rather than move today.`,
                    coach_r3_royalty_low:   `<strong>Haggling the royalty to near-zero wins the spreadsheet and loses the relationship.</strong> The spin-out is a renewable source of ideas, not a one-off vendor. Squeeze it now and the next deal — the medical enzyme — goes to someone who paid fairly. Partner-network value is built, not extracted.`,
                    coach_r3_royalty_high:  `<strong>A double-digit royalty gives away the value the licence was meant to capture.</strong> 3–8% is the band for enabling technology; agree to far more and Tritón's margin flows out the door you opened. Speed is worth paying for — but not at any price.`,
                    coach_r4_gov_full:      `<strong>That's an operating model, not a one-off deal.</strong> A named inbound-scouting function and an out-licensing desk on a quarterly cadence is what keeps the funnel open after you leave the room. Open innovation is an organisational capability — you've built the muscle, not just done the reps.`,
                    coach_r4_gov_scouting:  `<strong>An inbound desk is half the funnel.</strong> Scouting opens the front, but with no out-licensing desk the 98 dormant patents drift back to the shelf. “We'll worry about outbound later” is how the side door quietly re-closes. Staff both ends or you've only half-opened the funnel.`,
                    coach_r4_gov_none:      `<strong>No operating model means this reverts to closed the moment you leave.</strong> Who runs inbound scouting next quarter? Who staffs the out-licensing desk? Deals without governance are a one-time event — exactly how Lumen got here. The capability, not the contract, is the deliverable.`,
                    coach_r4_kpi_revenue:   `<strong>Total revenue alone won't tell the board openness is working.</strong> It moves for a hundred reasons and lags by years. Without external-idea yield, time-to-market and R&D ROI, no one can see whether the funnel actually opened — and what you can't measure, you can't sustain.`,
                    coach_r4_kpi_patents:   `<strong>Rewarding patents filed is paying for the disease.</strong> Lumen already files plenty and commercialises under 30% — more filings without commercialisation just grows the dormant shelf. The KPI has to reward ideas that <em>move</em>, in or out, not ideas that sit.`,
                    coach_r4_scalpel:       `<strong>That's the answer the board chair was testing for.</strong> Inbound for speed, outbound for dormant value, the anti-corrosion core defended — openness as a scalpel, strategy-led and selective. The technical edge is sharper for the external ideas you absorbed, not sold off. This is the turnaround you were sent to deliver.`,
                    coach_r4_closed_pride:  `<strong>“We protected everything” is defending the model that lost the race.</strong> An intact core means nothing if you arrive 14 months late again. The board didn't appoint you to keep the funnel shut more carefully — they appointed you to open it without losing the edge. Sealing it isn't a turnaround.`,
                    coach_r4_fire_sale:     `<strong>“We monetised aggressively” reads as a fire sale to the board.</strong> Licensing widely for cash, core included, funds this year and arms your competitors for the next. Openness that raises money by selling differentiation is the trap the chair was probing for. A scalpel, not a clearance sale.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
